sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/species-area-exponent--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Random-effects meta-analysis of z heterogeneity plus a METE scaling-collapse test" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/species-area-exponent> ;
    dct:description "Universality means the true between-dataset variance τ² of z is near zero after sampling error. Using the Triantis et al. 2012 and Drakare et al. 2006 compilations, estimate each dataset's z with its standard error (refitting from the raw S–A data where supplied, otherwise from reported fits) and fit a random-effects meta-regression: z_j = μ + x_j'β + u_j + e_j, with u_j ~ N(0, τ²), e_j ~ N(0, se_j²), and covariates for isolation, island type, taxon and body size. Pre-registered: universality refuted if τ's 95% CI excludes 0.03, a z standard deviation one eighth of the canonical 0.25. Independently, test METE's specific prediction (Harte et al. 2009) that the local slope z at a scale is a universal decreasing function of log(N/S) at that scale: for datasets with abundance N, compute local slopes and test the collapse onto the METE curve by its residual variance against a free-curve alternative." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "τ with CI (universality supported if the CI upper bound is < 0.03, refuted if the lower bound is > 0.03), the share of heterogeneity explained by covariates, and whether local slopes collapse onto the METE curve, the decisive test between 'universal exponent' and 'universal non-power form'." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Too few datasets report abundances for the METE test (fewer than 20), and z standard errors are unavailable for most compiled curves, making τ uninterpretable." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register τ threshold, covariates and the METE collapse statistic. 2. Extract per-dataset z and SE; flag datasets with abundance data for the METE test. 3. Fit the random-effects meta-regression (REML) and report τ², I² and covariate effects. 4. Run the METE collapse test on datasets with abundances; report the residual variance ratio." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Raw S–A data for many compiled curves are not public, so standard errors may have to be reconstructed from the reported number of islands and R²." ;
    skos:note "Tool: meta-analysis" ;
    skos:note "Tool: macroecology" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch12-04> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch12-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch11-02> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2699.2011.02652.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2005.00848.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2009.01328.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2005.00848.x> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/species-area-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Why does the species-area exponent cluster where it does?" .

  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2699.2011.02652.x> dct:title "Triantis et al. 2012 compilation" ;
    dct:description "Island SAR datasets and fits" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2005.00848.x> dct:title "Drakare et al. 2006 compilation" ;
    dct:description "794 species–area curves with design covariates" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2009.01328.x> dct:title "Biodiversity scales from plots to biomes with a universal species–area curve" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Harte et al. 2009, METE universal SAR" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2005.00848.x> dct:title "The imprint of the geographical, evolutionary and ecological context on species–area relationships" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Drakare, Lennon and Hillebrand 2006" .
}

